TLP automatically switches between high performance (AC profile) and battery savings (BAT profile) the moment you plug or unplug your power adapter.

To manually force the laptop into either mode, or to permanently tweak how aggressive these power profiles are, copy and run this block:

Bash
# ==============================================================================
# 1. TEMPORARY OVERRIDES (These last until you plug/unplug the power cord)
# ==============================================================================

# Check the current active TLP profile and system power state
# sudo : executes with root privileges
# tlp-stat : TLP's diagnostic and reporting tool
# -s : flag to filter output to just the basic system status and active profile
sudo tlp-stat -s

# Force TLP into Maximum Battery Savings mode immediately
# tlp : calls the main command-line utility
# bat : forces the 'battery' power profile, aggressively throttling components to save power
sudo tlp bat

# Force TLP into High Performance mode immediately
# ac : forces the 'AC' power profile, lifting thermal and power limits for maximum speed
sudo tlp ac

# Reset TLP back to Automatic mode
# start : re-evaluates the physical power source (plugged in or unplugged) and applies the correct default profile
sudo tlp start

# ==============================================================================
# 2. PERMANENT CONFIGURATION TWEAKS
# ==============================================================================

# Open the TLP configuration file to permanently change hardware behavior
# nano : a lightweight, terminal-based text editor
# /etc/tlp.conf : the primary configuration file containing all power rules
sudo nano /etc/tlp.conf

# NOTE: While inside nano, press Ctrl+W to search for the following lines. 
# Remove the '#' at the start of the line to enable them, and set your desired values.
#
# To control the overall laptop cooling and performance profile:
# PLATFORM_PROFILE_ON_AC=performance
# PLATFORM_PROFILE_ON_BAT=low-power
#
# To control how the Intel/AMD CPU scales its clock speeds:
# CPU_ENERGY_PERF_POLICY_ON_AC=performance
# CPU_ENERGY_PERF_POLICY_ON_BAT=power
#
# After editing, press Ctrl+O then Enter to save, and Ctrl+X to exit nano.

# Restart the TLP background service to permanently apply any changes made to the config file
# systemctl : the control interface for systemd services
# restart : stops and immediately starts the service to load the new settings into memory
# tlp.service : the specific power management daemon
sudo systemctl restart tlp.service
Verification: After running sudo tlp bat or sudo tlp ac, run sudo tlp-stat -s again. Look at the bottom of the output for Mode = battery or Mode = AC to confirm the profile successfully switched.